<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>A worked example: 2 tickets listed at $200</h2>
    <p>Using this calculator's default fee assumptions, here is what one $200 ticket pays on each site, and roughly what a buyer sees at checkout:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th class="num">Seller fee</th><th class="num">You keep</th><th class="num">Buyer pays (est.)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Ticketmaster Resale</td><td class="num">10%</td><td class="num">$180.00</td><td class="num">$236.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SeatGeek</td><td class="num">12%</td><td class="num">$176.00</td><td class="num">$230.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>StubHub</td><td class="num">15%</td><td class="num">$170.00</td><td class="num">$244.00</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>For the pair, the gap between the best and worst payout is $20. That gap is not the whole story: the same $200 listing costs a buyer $244 on StubHub but $230 on SeatGeek, so where you list also changes how competitive your seats look.</p>

    <h2>Price against what buyers actually see</h2>
    <p>Since the FTC's rule on unfair or deceptive fees took effect on May 12, 2025, ticket sites must show buyers the total price, including mandatory fees, up front. Buyers compare all-in prices, so your listing competes on list price plus the platform's buyer fee, not on list price alone. Before setting a price, look at the all-in price of similar seats on the site where you plan to list and work backward to your list price.</p>
    <p>Fees also vary. StubHub says its seller fee depends on price, demand, and timing, and Ticketmaster Resale fees are set per event. Treat the defaults here as a starting point and replace them with the fee shown in your listing preview.</p>

    <h2>Setting a floor so you don't sell at a loss</h2>
    <p>Start with what you paid per ticket, including the original service fees. Say that was $120. To get $120 back after fees, the minimum list prices are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ticketmaster Resale (10%):</strong> $133.33</li>
      <li><strong>SeatGeek (12%):</strong> $136.36</li>
      <li><strong>StubHub (15%):</strong> $141.18</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Choose "Net a target amount", enter $120, and fill in "What you paid (each)" to see this for your own tickets. The formula is target ÷ (1 − seller fee): at 15%, every dollar you want to keep requires about $1.18 of list price.</p>

    <h2>Timing and the rules that come before price</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Check whether the ticket can be resold at all.</strong> Some events limit resale to face value, restrict transfers, or allow resale only on the original seller's exchange.</li>
      <li><strong>Unsold tickets have no value after the event starts.</strong> If the price is not moving as the date gets close, a lower price that still clears your floor beats an unsold ticket.</li>
      <li><strong>Listing on several sites at once</strong> risks selling the same seat twice. If you cross-list, remove the other listings the moment one sells.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep your purchase receipt.</strong> Resale gains can be taxable income, and marketplaces may report your sales to the IRS. Losses on tickets bought for personal use generally are not deductible. A tax professional can tell you how this applies to you.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Ticket resale questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How much does StubHub take when you sell a ticket?</summary><p>This calculator assumes a 15% seller fee for StubHub, which pays $170 on a $200 listing. StubHub says its actual fee varies with price, demand, and timing, so use the payout shown in your listing preview to confirm.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What should I list my tickets for to get my money back?</summary><p>Divide what you paid by 1 minus the seller fee. To recover $120 at a 15% fee, list at $141.18 or more. At 10%, $133.33 is enough.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Do buyers see my list price or a higher price?</summary><p>Buyers see the total including the platform's mandatory buyer fees. Under the FTC's fee rule, ticket sites must show that all-in price up front, so a $200 listing can appear as $230 to $244 depending on the site.</p></details>
  </section>
